            {
              "id": "a920f2cf-15c7-3a08-91df-fb0c2c5c0b46",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `vsnprintf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of using `vsnprintf`, consider using `snprintf` with a maximum size argument to prevent buffer overflow. Also, always validate and sanitize input data to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `vsnprintf` function with `snprintf` function:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(filename_, sizeof(filename_), \"%s\", filename);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a578f881-0436-379b-901f-304f0042d884",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function Vulnerability\" is a common security issue in C++ programming language. It occurs when a programmer uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential for causing vulnerabilities. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or file-related vulnerabilities.\n\nThe `fopen` function is used to open a file, but it does not perform any validation on the filename. This can lead to a vulnerability if an attacker can control the filename, as they could potentially open any file on the system.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using `fopen` and instead use safer alternatives that perform better validation. If `fopen` must be used, ensure that the filename is properly validated and sanitized to prevent an attacker from opening arbitrary files.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fopen`, consider using `fstream` from the `<fstream>` library, which is a safer alternative. Here is an example of how to use `fstream` to open a file:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(filename_, std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fc8434e6-e9df-3388-8b8e-2ccedd94019e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `vsnprintf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In C++, the Standard Template Library (STL) provides many safe alternatives. For example, instead of using `vsnprintf`, you can use `std::snprintf` which is safer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstdarg>\n\nvoid safe_function(const char* name_, ...) {\n    char name[rocsparseio_string];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, name_);\n    std::vsnprintf(name, sizeof(name), name_, args);\n    va_end(args);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- cstdio\n- cstdarg\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
