            {
              "id": "69281d28-c9fb-3057-aed4-ef4279cf26f3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The destination is a character pointer, and the source is an element from an argument vector, with dataflow analysis indicating that the destination may overlap with the source. This overlap can cause undefined behavior according to the C standard for strncpy. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy. The source and destination are both considered non-null, reducing the likelihood of a false positive. The classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence issue that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when using `strncpy`, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check before the copy to verify that the memory regions are distinct:\n\n```cpp\nif (t_ != this->argv[k]) {\n    strncpy(t_, this->argv[k], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n} else {\n    // Handle the overlap case appropriately, e.g., skip copy or use a temporary buffer\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if overlapping memory regions are possible and must be supported, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is defined for overlapping regions:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(t_, this->argv[k], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n```\n\nNote: `memmove` does not guarantee null-termination, so if a null-terminated string is required, ensure to set the last byte to `\\0` after the copy if needed.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6c0db8bc-d44e-3cfd-b90e-320c66db5fc6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy operation where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This is indicated by the analysis, which detected that both source and destination expressions are derived from the same base object, increasing the risk of undefined behavior. The risk is further elevated because there are no explicit checks or guards to prevent overlap, and the function used (strncpy) does not handle overlapping memory regions safely. The absence of explicit null-termination after the copy and the lack of bounds checks further increase the risk. Although the source and destination are not null at this point, the main concern is the potential for memory overlap, which can lead to unpredictable program behavior or security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying between potentially overlapping memory regions, add a runtime check to ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling the copy function. If overlap is possible, use a safe copy method or handle the overlap explicitly.\n\n```cpp\n// Before calling strncpy, check for overlap\nchar* dst = this->argv[j + i];\nchar* src = this->argv[k + 1 + i];\nsize_t len = (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN;\nif ((dst + len <= src) || (src + len <= dst)) {\n    // No overlap, safe to use strncpy\n    strncpy(dst, src, len);\n} else {\n    // Overlap detected, handle appropriately (e.g., use memmove or copy to temp buffer)\n    memmove(dst, src, len);\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, replace the use of `strncpy` with `memmove`, which is designed to safely handle overlapping memory regions. This change ensures that the copy operation is well-defined even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n```cpp\n// Use memmove instead of strncpy for overlapping regions\nchar* dst = this->argv[j + i];\nchar* src = this->argv[k + 1 + i];\nsize_t len = (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN;\nmemmove(dst, src, len);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "47b98e33-ae21-3ae1-b06c-aed16bc27219",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard library function in C++ for converting a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not perform any error checking. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform error checking, such as `strtol()` or `sscanf()`. Also, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi(argv[3])` with the following code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for strtol\n#include <cerrno>  // for errno\n\nchar *end;\nlong val = strtol(argv[3], &end, 10);\n\nif (end == argv[3] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle conversion error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- cstdlib\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c43be23f-5e9b-32a9-9a90-30af315823f2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C library functions that are considered unsafe. These functions are unsafe because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary validation, which can lead to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows.\n\nIn this specific case, the function `atoi()` is used, which converts a string to an integer. `atoi()` does not check for overflow or underflow, and it does not signal any error if the input string does not represent a valid integer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()`. Instead, use functions that perform error checking, such as `strtol()`, or use C++ style input/output, which can throw exceptions on error.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi(argv[2])` with the following code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar *endptr;\nlong val = strtol(argv[2], &endptr, 10);\n\nif (errno == ERANGE || *endptr != '\\0' || endptr == argv[2]) {\n    // Handle the error...\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `strtol()` is used to convert the string to a long integer. It sets `errno` to `ERANGE` on overflow or underflow, and it sets `endptr` to point to any remaining characters after the number.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `strtol()`\n- `<cerrno>`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f421ab01-42c7-39ad-b801-27005c711510",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses a function that can lead to a security risk. In C++, certain functions are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, integer overflows, or other types of vulnerabilities. One such function is `atoi()`, which converts a string to an integer. This function does not perform any error checking, so if the string cannot be converted into an integer, it will return a zero or a negative value, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `atoi()` and instead use functions that perform error checking, such as `strtol()`. This function allows you to specify the base for the conversion and it sets `errno` to `ERANGE` if the number cannot be represented.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi(argv[1])` with the following code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n#include <climits>\n\nchar *endptr;\nlong val = strtol(argv[1], &endptr, 10);\n\nif ((errno == ERANGE && (val == LONG_MAX || val == LONG_MIN)) || (errno != 0 && val == 0)) {\n    perror(\"strtol\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n}\n\nif (endptr == argv[1]) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"No digits were found\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- cstdlib\n- cerrno\n- climits\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a4aff05a-748d-3b61-b3b7-bad0fc90e885",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses the strncpy function to copy data between elements of the same array (argv), where the source and destination may overlap. This can result in undefined behavior according to the C standard, as strncpy does not support overlapping memory regions. The analysis specifically identified that both source and destination share the same base object (this), and there is no evidence of runtime checks or guards to prevent overlap. Additionally, there are no explicit null-termination steps after the copy, and the maximum length is provided as a constant, not derived from the actual buffer size. The risk of undefined behavior is high in this context, and the finding is not mitigated by any other safety checks.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying between potentially overlapping memory regions, add a runtime check to ensure that the source and destination do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. If overlap is possible, use a temporary buffer to safely copy the data:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->argv[k + i] != this->argv[k + 1 + i]) {\n    strncpy(this->argv[k + i], this->argv[k + 1 + i], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n} else {\n    // No action needed if source and destination are the same\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, if overlap is possible and you cannot guarantee non-overlapping regions, use a temporary buffer:\n\n```cpp\nchar temp[rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN];\nmemcpy(temp, this->argv[k + 1 + i], rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\nstrncpy(this->argv[k + i], temp, rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and you want to avoid undefined behavior, replace `strncpy` with `memmove`, which is safe for overlapping regions. Note that `memmove` does not guarantee null-termination, so you may need to add it explicitly:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(this->argv[k + i], this->argv[k + 1 + i], rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n// Optionally ensure null-termination if required\nthis->argv[k + i][rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "829af352-df29-3442-b29b-31fc0df6356c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The source and destination expressions both reference elements of the same array or structure (as indicated by the use of 'this->argv' with different indices), which can result in overlapping memory if the indices are not sufficiently separated. The classification explicitly states a risk of undefined behavior, and the verdict is marked as genuine. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and there is no evidence that the source or destination pointers are null at this point. The count argument is a constant, but this does not mitigate the overlap risk. Therefore, this is a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent potential memory corruption or unpredictable program behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying between potentially overlapping memory regions, add a runtime check to ensure that the source and destination do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use a temporary buffer to safely copy the data:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->argv[k + i] != this->argv[k + 1 + i]) {\n    strncpy(this->argv[k + i], this->argv[k + 1 + i], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n} else {\n    // If overlap is possible, use a temporary buffer\n    char tmp[rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN];\n    strncpy(tmp, this->argv[k + 1 + i], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n    strncpy(this->argv[k + i], tmp, (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, replace the use of `strncpy` with `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(this->argv[k + i], this->argv[k + 1 + i], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3e76fb9c-6c22-3476-bb95-6fed9858b399",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard library function in C++ for converting a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not perform any error checking. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()` function. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform error checking, such as `strtol()` or `sscanf()`. Also, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi(argv[3])` with the following code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for strtol\n#include <cerrno>  // for errno\n\nchar *end;\nlong val = strtol(argv[3], &end, 10);\n\nif (end == argv[3] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle conversion error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- cstdlib\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
