            {
              "id": "a3146d42-a81d-3092-b639-510558cadeb3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to a vulnerability if the string is not null-terminated. The `strlen` function counts characters until it encounters a null character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, which can lead to buffer overflows, crashes, or other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your string. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read beyond this length.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nlen = strlen(name);\n```\n\nYou can use:\n\n```cpp\nlen = strnlen(name, MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum expected length of your string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE-126)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dd598ab6-1d6c-31cf-94f4-ffb58e1a988a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to a vulnerability if the string is not null-terminated. The `strlen` function counts characters until it encounters a null character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, which can lead to buffer overflows, crashes, or other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your string. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read beyond this length.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nlen = strlen(name);\n```\n\nYou can use:\n\n```cpp\nlen = strnlen(name, MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum expected length of your string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE-126)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6295c519-3fdb-3a0c-9302-558248720022",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow if the input string represents a number outside the range of representable integer values.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other forms of validation to prevent vulnerabilities. In this case, consider using `strtol()` or `sscanf()` instead of `atoi()`. Always validate and sanitize input data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi(getenv_str)` with the following code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <climits>\n\nconst char* getenv_str = std::getenv(\"ENV_VAR\");\nlong val = 0;\n\nif (getenv_str != nullptr) {\n    char* end;\n    val = std::strtol(getenv_str, &end, 10);\n    if (end == getenv_str || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n        // handle conversion error\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- cstdlib\n- climits\n- errno\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-681: Incorrect Conversion between Numeric Types](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/681.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d62be451-0c07-3112-823c-8b804e6c2b5d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict the outcome of the random number generator, leading to a potential security breach.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the code example is the use of `std::shuffle` with a non-cryptographic or weak random number generator.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved using the `<random>` library which provides a variety of random number generators that are suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix for the vulnerability sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 g(rd());\n\nstd::shuffle(&coo_col_ind[start], &coo_col_ind[end], g);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to seed the Mersenne Twister engine (`std::mt19937`), which is a cryptographically secure random number generator. This generator is then used with `std::shuffle`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`: for `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`\n- `<algorithm>`: for `std::shuffle`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ecf13cfc-1c38-351d-9920-de6c2cbe9965",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict the outcome of the random number generator, leading to a potential security breach.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `std::shuffle` is used with a potentially weak random number generator, `generator`. This could lead to predictable shuffling of the `csr_col_ind` array, which could be a security risk if the shuffled array is used in a security-critical context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved using the `<random>` library's `std::random_device` or other cryptographic libraries like OpenSSL's random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `std::random_device`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n#include <algorithm>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 g(rd());\n\nstd::shuffle(&csr_col_ind[start], &csr_col_ind[end], g);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`: for `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`\n- `<algorithm>`: for `std::shuffle`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7eaa8916-6032-35d4-9ba2-3fe3dfe3de28",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the filename is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to ensure that inputs are properly validated and sanitized. In the case of `fopen`, it is important to ensure that the filename is not controlled by an untrusted user, or if it is, that it is properly validated and sanitized to prevent directory traversal attacks or other types of file system attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(filename);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the C++ `ifstream` class is used instead of `fopen`. This class automatically handles many of the issues associated with `fopen`, such as buffer overflows.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8eed4c59-8bef-354b-a56f-7ee98c7a2d34",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The copy length is a fixed value and does not appear to be derived from the actual capacity of the destination buffer, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can also lead to string handling errors. All these factors indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the pointers are not null and that the memory regions are distinct. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_msg != nullptr && msg_ != nullptr &&\n    (this->m_msg + 256 <= msg_ || msg_ + 256 <= this->m_msg)) {\n    strncpy(this->m_msg, msg_, 256);\n    this->m_msg[255] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the source and destination do not overlap and that both pointers are valid. It also guarantees that the destination buffer is null-terminated.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as the memory move function. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_msg != nullptr && msg_ != nullptr) {\n    memmove(this->m_msg, msg_, 256);\n    this->m_msg[255] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination if copying strings\n}\n```\nThis function safely handles overlapping memory regions and avoids undefined behavior. However, be aware that this function does not guarantee null-termination, so you should explicitly set the last byte to the null character if you are copying strings.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9839e413-2ecd-3328-8730-ef160b80f0f1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses a C library function that is considered unsafe. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to buffer overflows, integer overflows, or other types of vulnerabilities that can be exploited by an attacker.\n\nIn this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which converts a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not perform any error checking. If the string cannot be converted to an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior if not handled properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the `atoi()` function with a safer alternative that performs error checking. In C++, you can use the `std::stoi()` function, which throws an exception if the conversion fails.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can replace `atoi()` with `std::stoi()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\ntry {\n    int layer_mode = std::stoi(str_layer_mode);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {\n    // Handle exception\n} catch (std::out_of_range& e) {\n    // Handle exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`\n- `<cstdlib>`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "87a3fda7-af65-3c7c-aba3-f5b56b02c6f2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, which increases the risk of null pointer dereference. The count argument is a literal value (128), but there is no evidence that this matches the actual capacity of the destination buffer, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy. These factors together indicate a real risk of memory corruption or undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the pointers are not null and that the memory regions are distinct. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_function_filename != nullptr && function_filename_ != nullptr &&\n    (this->m_function_filename + 128 <= function_filename_ || function_filename_ + 128 <= this->m_function_filename)) {\n    strncpy(this->m_function_filename, function_filename_, 128);\n    this->m_function_filename[127] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the regions do not overlap and that the destination is always null-terminated.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use the `memmove` function instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping regions. Also, ensure null-termination:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_function_filename != nullptr && function_filename_ != nullptr) {\n    memmove(this->m_function_filename, function_filename_, 128);\n    this->m_function_filename[127] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "371585ab-d471-33f0-a812-9d6c28b4a438",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the strncpy function and can lead to unpredictable results. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of a guard for buffer sizes or null checks further increases the risk. The classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the copy operation, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the pointers do not refer to overlapping memory. Also, check that neither pointer is null before performing the copy.\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_function_name != nullptr && function_name_ != nullptr &&\n    (this->m_function_name + 128 <= function_name_ || function_name_ + 128 <= this->m_function_name)) {\n    strncpy(this->m_function_name, function_name_, 128);\n    this->m_function_name[127] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the memory regions do not overlap and that both pointers are valid.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use a function that is safe for overlapping memory, such as memmove. However, be aware that memmove does not handle null-terminated strings, so you must ensure proper null-termination after the copy.\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_function_name != nullptr && function_name_ != nullptr) {\n    memmove(this->m_function_name, function_name_, 128);\n    this->m_function_name[127] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nThis approach avoids undefined behavior even if the memory regions overlap, but you must still ensure that the destination buffer is large enough and properly null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c4970af4-bd51-3e51-b65d-1e03641e91e4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of the block is specified in bytes. If the allocation is successful, a pointer to the block of memory is returned. If the allocation fails, a NULL pointer is returned. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size parameter passed to `malloc()` is not properly validated or is manipulated in such a way that it can cause an overflow or underflow condition. This can lead to unexpected behavior, including memory corruption, crashes, or potentially code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is important to validate all inputs that are used to determine the size of the memory allocation. This includes ensuring that the size is not zero, not excessively large, and not based on untrusted user input. \n\nAdditionally, it is important to check the return value of `malloc()` to ensure that the allocation was successful before using the memory. If `malloc()` returns a NULL pointer, this indicates that the allocation failed and the program should handle this error condition appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Assuming 'that.m' is an integer representing the size\nif (that.m > 0) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(that.m);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle allocation failure\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9eb7c686-99ad-3110-a0f7-c676ca1239ae",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of the block is specified in bytes. If the allocation is successful, a pointer to the block of memory is returned. If the allocation fails, a NULL pointer is returned. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size parameter passed to `malloc()` is not properly validated or is manipulated in such a way that it can cause an overflow or underflow condition. This can lead to unexpected behavior, including memory corruption, crashes, or potentially code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is important to validate all inputs that are used to determine the size of the memory allocation. This includes ensuring that the size is not zero, not excessively large, and not based on untrusted user input. \n\nAdditionally, it is important to check the return value of `malloc()` to ensure that the allocation was successful before using the memory. If `malloc()` returns a NULL pointer, this indicates that the allocation failed and the program should handle this error condition appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Assuming 'that.m' is an integer representing the size\nif (that.m > 0) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(that.m);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle allocation failure\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0b5a97c0-cdf3-34e3-a518-62246eb1c941",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If not used properly, it can lead to vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, memory leaks, and null pointer dereferencing. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code, cause a Denial of Service (DoS), or read sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always check the return value of `malloc()`. If the system is out of memory, `malloc()` will return a null pointer. Using this null pointer will lead to undefined behavior.\n\n2. Always initialize the memory returned by `malloc()`. Uninitialized memory can contain sensitive information.\n\n3. Always free the memory allocated by `malloc()` when it is no longer needed. Failing to do so will lead to memory leaks.\n\n4. Be careful with the size argument passed to `malloc()`. If the size is not calculated correctly, it can lead to buffer overflows or integer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to use `malloc()` properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint* allocateArray(int size) {\n    if (size <= 0) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    int* array = (int*) malloc(size * sizeof(int));\n\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    // Initialize array\n    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n        array[i] = 0;\n    }\n\n    return array;\n}\n\nvoid deallocateArray(int* array) {\n    free(array);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we first check if the size is valid. Then we allocate the memory and check if `malloc()` succeeded. We initialize the array to 0 and return it. When we are done with the array, we free the memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [CWE-401: Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/401.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "71159834-69f2-3857-82bd-8fc325e04a1b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the string copy operation. The code copies data from one buffer to another using a string copy function, but dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory areas, which is not allowed by the standard and can lead to unpredictable results. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are not null, which could result in a crash or further undefined behavior. The absence of a null-terminator assignment after the copy further increases the risk of string-related errors. These factors indicate a real risk that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Add explicit checks to verify that the memory regions are distinct and that neither pointer is null. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_arg_name != nullptr && arg_name_ != nullptr &&\n    (this->m_arg_name + 128 <= arg_name_ || arg_name_ + 128 <= this->m_arg_name)) {\n    strncpy(this->m_arg_name, arg_name_, 128);\n    this->m_arg_name[127] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the buffers do not overlap and are valid. Always ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated after using strncpy.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf it is possible that the source and destination buffers may overlap, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as memmove. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->m_arg_name != nullptr && arg_name_ != nullptr) {\n    memmove(this->m_arg_name, arg_name_, 128);\n    this->m_arg_name[127] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination if copying strings\n}\n```\nNote: memmove is safe for overlapping memory, but you must still ensure proper null-termination if you are copying strings.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c907bd25-6dfa-302b-98cb-9f92c4176a95",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of the block is specified in bytes. If the allocation is successful, a pointer to the block of memory is returned. If the allocation fails, a NULL pointer is returned. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size parameter passed to `malloc()` is not properly validated or is manipulated in such a way that it can cause an overflow or underflow condition. This can lead to unexpected behavior, including memory corruption, crashes, or potentially code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is important to validate all inputs that are used to determine the size of the memory allocation. This includes ensuring that the size is not zero, not excessively large, and not based on untrusted user input. \n\nAdditionally, it is important to check the return value of `malloc()` to ensure that the allocation was successful before using the memory. If `malloc()` returns a NULL pointer, this indicates that the allocation failed and the program should handle this error condition appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Assuming 'that.m' is an integer representing the size\nif (that.m > 0) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(that.m);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle allocation failure\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "58429419-ed8c-31b5-a766-862bf32b3ac2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size parameter passed to `malloc` is not properly validated or is manipulated in such a way that it can cause a buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other memory corruption issues. This can lead to unpredictable program behavior, crashes, or even code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate the size parameter before passing it to `malloc`.\n2. Avoid using user-supplied or externally influenced numbers directly in memory allocation functions.\n3. Use safer memory management functions if possible, such as those provided by the C++ Standard Library (e.g., `new` and `delete`).\n4. Implement error checking after every `malloc` call. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer, which should be checked.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to fix the vulnerability in the given code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Assuming m_ is an integer\nint m_ = 10;\n\n// Validate m_ before using it in malloc\nif (m_ > 0) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(size_t(m_));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    } else {\n        // Continue with program\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d842b41e-1ce3-3ee9-b7d5-128e6118c37e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the input is not properly validated or sanitized. An attacker could potentially exploit this to read sensitive files or execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or ensure proper input validation and error handling. In the case of `fopen`, it is important to validate the input file path and handle errors properly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `fstream` in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\n    std::ifstream file(argv[1]);\n    if (!file) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n    // Rest of the code\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
